Step 1: Initial Setup and Disk Examination

First, we unzip the provided disk image and examine its structure:
bash

# Unzip the disk image
gunzip disk.img.gz

# Check the file type
file disk.img
# Output shows it's a DOS/MBR boot sector with 3 partitions

# Examine partition layout
fdisk -l disk.img

The disk has three partitions:

    Partition 1: Linux filesystem (startsector 2048, 300M)

    Partition 2: Linux swap (startsector 616448, 256M)

    Partition 3: Linux filesystem (startsector 1140736, 467M)

Step 2: Mount the Root Filesystem

We need to mount partition 3, which likely contains the root filesystem:
bash

# Create mount points
sudo mkdir -p /mnt/disk1 /mnt/disk3

# First attempt to mount partition 3 fails because partition 1 is mounted
sudo mount -o loop,offset=$((2048*512)) disk.img /mnt/disk1

# Unmount partition 1
sudo umount /mnt/disk1

# Mount partition 3 (the root filesystem)
sudo mount -o loop,offset=$((1140736*512)) disk.img /mnt/disk3

Step 3: Analyze with The Sleuth Kit

Using forensic tools to examine the filesystem:
bash

# Install sleuthkit if needed
sudo apt install sleuthkit -y

# List files in partition 3
fls -o 1140736 disk.img

# Recursively search for Git-related content
fls -o 1140736 -r disk.img | grep -i git

The output reveals interesting findings:

    A .git directory at inode 65665

    Various Git binaries and related files throughout the system

Step 4: Locate the Git Repository

Search for the actual Git repository on the mounted filesystem:
bash

# Find all .git directories
find /mnt/disk3 -name ".git" -type d 2>/dev/null

Output reveals: /mnt/disk3/home/ctf-player/Code/secrets/.git
Step 5: Explore the Git Repository

Navigate to the repository and examine its history:
bash

cd /mnt/disk3/home/ctf-player/Code/secrets
ls -la

View the Git log to see commit history:
bash

git log --oneline

Sample output might show:
text

2a9e3a7 (HEAD -> main) Add secret file
f7b8c9d Initial commit

Examine the latest commit:
bash

git show

